# Postgres. The app runs on Neon; nothing here needs Docker.
#
#   neon link --project-id <id>            pins the project in .neon
#   neon checkout development --create     makes a development branch and writes
#                                          DATABASE_URL and the NEON_* and AWS_*
#                                          values below into this file
#
# A pooled DATABASE_URL (a `-pooler` host) is correct for the running app.
# Prisma's CLI derives the direct host from it, so migrations need no second
# variable. A local Postgres works too: point DATABASE_URL at it.
DATABASE_URL=""

# The branch DATABASE_URL belongs to. `neon checkout` keeps it in step. A
# protected name (production, prod, main, live) makes every development database
# command refuse, with no override.
# NEON_BRANCH="development"

# `db:migrate`, `db:push`, `db:reset`, `db:seed` and `dev` refuse a database that
# is not on this machine. Name the host of a development branch here to allow
# it. Comma-separated hostnames, pooled or direct form.
# DEV_DATABASE_HOSTS=""

# Hosts no development command may ever touch, even with ALLOW_REMOTE_DB=1 and
# even when a host is also listed above. Name the production host here.
# `db:deploy` refuses these too, unless a release build runs it or
# ALLOW_PRODUCTION_MIGRATE=1 confirms it.
# PROTECTED_DATABASE_HOSTS=""

# Optional. Sizes the Postgres pool of each process. On serverless every
# instance has its own pool, so keep instances times the maximum under the
# database connection limit.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX="10"
# DATABASE_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS="30000"
# DATABASE_POOL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"

# A throwaway database for `bun run test`. The suite will not touch DATABASE_URL
# and refuses to run without this — these are real integration tests, they write
# and delete rows, and the pre-push hook runs them. The name has to end in
# `_test`. `bun run db:test` creates it and applies the migrations.
#
# A local Postgres is fastest. A `crm_test` database on a Neon development
# branch works without Docker and takes a network round trip per query: use the
# branch's DATABASE_URL with the database name changed to `crm_test`.
TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/crm_test?schema=public"

# Generate your own:  openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""

# Optional. Who may create an account. Leave it empty for self-service sign-up:
# anyone can register, and a new account joins no workspace until it creates
# its own agency workspace or accepts an invitation. Workspace membership — not
# this list — decides which data a person can see.
#
# Set it to restrict an install, for example a private self-host or a
# development box. Comma-separated, each entry a whole email domain or a single
# address; subdomains count:
#
#   ALLOWED_SIGN_IN="acme.com"                       only your company
#   ALLOWED_SIGN_IN="acme.com,contractor@gmail.com"  …plus one outsider
ALLOWED_SIGN_IN=""

# Optional. "true" turns on email-and-password accounts, which is how you sign
# in locally without a Google client. There is no email verification, so an
# unverified password account cannot accept an invitation, and a password
# account is never linked to a Google or Microsoft sign-in for the same
# address. Leave it off in production.
# AUTH_EMAIL_PASSWORD="true"

# Google is the sign-in method a clone starts with. Signing in asks only for
# name and email; reading Gmail and Calendar is a separate, optional grant on
# Settings → Connections. Set both or neither — half a pair is a sign-in button
# that fails at Google.
#
# Leave them empty only if you sign in with Microsoft below, or with your own
# identity provider added on Settings → SSO. With none of the three there is no
# way in at all, and the sign-in page says as much rather than showing you
# nothing.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Microsoft 365 / Entra ID — the other sign-in method, and the same app
# registration is what reads Outlook mail. Set both or neither, exactly like
# the Google pair.
#
# Create the app at https://portal.azure.com → Microsoft Entra ID → App
# registrations, add the redirect URI <API_URL>/api/auth/callback/microsoft,
# and give it the delegated Graph permissions User.Read and Mail.Read. The
# README has the full walkthrough.
#
# You can set Google and Microsoft together: the sign-in page offers both, and
# a rep's mail is read from whichever they signed in with.
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=""
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Optional. Enables Slack account linking on Settings > Connections.
# Add APP_URL + /api/auth/oauth2/callback/slack as the Slack OAuth redirect URL.
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID=""
# SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Which Entra tenant may sign in. "common" (the default) accepts any work,
# school or personal Microsoft account and leans on ALLOWED_SIGN_IN to decide
# who actually gets in; your own tenant's GUID refuses everyone else at
# Microsoft, before they ever reach us. "organizations" allows any work or
# school account but no personal ones.
# MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID="common"

# Email. Sends the sign-in link and workspace invitations through Resend.
# Without the key, production offers no email sign-in and creates invitations
# without sending them. Outside production the message is printed to the API
# log instead, so a local sign-in still completes.
# https://resend.com/api-keys
# RESEND_API_KEY=""
# EMAIL_FROM="Custom CRM <hello@example.com>"


# ── Where things are ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed when you deploy. The defaults below are the localhost ones.

# The API. Also the origin that mints session cookies and serves /api/auth/*.
# API_URL="http://localhost:3001"

# The web app. Comma-separate if it is served from more than one origin —
# the first is canonical, and the rest are simply allowed to call the API.
# APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Only when the API and the app are on different subdomains of one parent,
# e.g. api.example.com and app.example.com — then set ".example.com" so one
# session cookie covers both.
# AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=""

# The research agent, which is its own deployment. The API reads this too, to
# tell the agent a logo or a photograph is waiting rather than letting it find
# out on its next minute.
# AGENT_URL="http://127.0.0.1:2000"

# Port used by the self-hosted Eve service. Hosted runtimes can keep injecting
# their standard PORT variable instead.
# AGENT_PORT="2000"

# Lets a signed-in rep talk to the agent from the contact sheet.
#
# The browser never calls the agent directly. The app proxies /eve/v1/* on its
# own origin, checks the session, and mints a two-minute token signed with this
# secret; the agent verifies it and learns *which rep* is asking. Set the same
# value for both processes.  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# It also authorises two calls the API makes to the agent: the dispatch poke,
# which is what makes a new company's logo appear as it is added instead of on
# the next minute's tick, and the website lookup in onboarding.
#
# Leave it unset and the Agent tab reports that it is not configured, the poke
# is skipped rather than sent unauthenticated, and onboarding asks for the
# business details by hand. Nothing else changes: the agent still runs on its
# own schedule.
# AGENT_BRIDGE_SECRET=""

# Optional. Enables encrypted OAuth tokens for MCP connections.
# Generate a 32-byte key with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Leave it unset to disable MCP connections without affecting other features.
# MCP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# Optional. Encrypts agent builder chat events before database storage.
# Generate a 32-byte key with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Leave it unset to store sanitized builder chat events as plaintext.
# CHAT_EVENT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# PORT="3001"


# ── Optional: what the agent can do ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The research agent works with none of these — it falls back to what the CRM
# already knows (your own email and calendar history) and simply reports what
# it could not check. Each key you add unlocks one more place it can look, and
# it tells you at startup which ones are on.

# Context.dev — company brand data, people enrichment and web research. One
# platform key serves every workspace. Customers never supply a key and never
# see this one. https://link.context.dev/crm
# CONTEXT_DEV_API_KEY=""

# Research credits. Each workspace has a monthly allowance of provider credits,
# and the whole install has a monthly ceiling. A request that does not fit is
# refused before the provider is called. Defaults: 500 and 20000.
# RESEARCH_WORKSPACE_MONTHLY_CREDITS="500"
# RESEARCH_PLATFORM_MONTHLY_CREDITS="20000"

# Perplexity — finds where a person lives on the web, and recent news worth
# knowing before a call. https://perplexity.ai/settings/api
# PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=""

# GitHub — raises the rate limit when matching contacts to GitHub profiles.
# Any classic token with no scopes will do.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=""

# Vercel Blob — where every logo and profile picture in the CRM is kept.
#
# The URLs these arrive on are somebody else's: LinkedIn signs theirs with an
# expiry a few weeks out, a brand CDN is a rate limit on a page that draws forty
# logos, and a company's own favicon is served by a company with no idea we are
# drawing it. So the bytes are copied once and the record points at our copy.
#
# Read by the agent, by the API — which writes a favicon and a signed-in user's
# Google avatar — and by the seed. Not by the Next.js app, which only needs to
# recognise one of our URLs to route it through the image optimizer.
#
# Without it, a contact's photograph is not stored at all, because a URL that
# works today and 404s next month is worse than initials and nobody can tell
# which it will be. Everything else — logos, favicons, avatars — keeps the
# origin's URL and renders as an ordinary hotlinked image.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=""

# S3-compatible storage — the same job as Vercel Blob, on Neon Object Storage or
# any S3 API. All five are needed, and S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET wins over the Blob token
# when both are set. The bucket must allow anonymous reads, because the record
# stores the object's public URL: `<endpoint>/<bucket>/<key>`. On Neon the first
# four come from `neon deploy`, and `neon.ts` declares the `assets` bucket.
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3=""
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
# AWS_REGION=""
# S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET=""

# Neon Realtime — live updates on Settings → Members. The API seals one query
# per request with the secret; the browser receives the URL and the sealed
# query, never the secret. Without both, the page refreshes on its own actions
# only. Realtime cannot read a table with row-level security, so no tenant table
# is live through it: research and enrichment status reach the browser through
# the API's own event stream instead. Both come from `neon deploy`.
# NEON_REALTIME_URL=""
# NEON_REALTIME_SECRET=""

# Neon Functions — `neon/cron.ts` calls the API's `/internal/*` routes on the
# schedules in `apps/api/vercel.json`. Set this to the public API URL, then run
# `neon deploy --env .env`. Leave it unset when Vercel runs the crons, or each
# job runs twice. Unset, the function deploys with every trigger disabled.
# CRON_TARGET_URL=""

# Anthropic, called directly — no gateway or router in between. The agent, the
# workspace setup proposal and the copilot all use it. Server-side only: never
# expose it to the browser. Without it the CRM still works: setup falls back to
# a built-in agency template and the AI features say they are off.
# https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# Optional. The everyday model, and the model for harder reasoning such as the
# agent builder. Native Anthropic ids; the defaults are below.
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL="claude-haiku-5-5"
# ANTHROPIC_COMPLEX_MODEL="claude-opus-5-5"

# Optional, for `bun run db:seed`. The seed fills a "Demo Agency" workspace with
# placeholder reps. Name your own account here, after you have signed in once,
# to be added to it as an owner.
# SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=""


# ── Optional: operations ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Shared cache. Without it each instance caches in its own memory, which is
# correct — just not shared. Recommended in production once website tracking is
# on: the compiled tracking config, the collector's rate limit and the hourly
# cap on contacts created from forms are all counted here, and per-instance
# counters let a multi-instance deployment exceed both.
# REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6379"
# CACHE_TTL_MS="60000"

# Bearer token guarding POST /internal/sync/google, the Gmail/Calendar cron
# route, and POST /internal/tracking/retention, the nightly sweep that deletes
# tracked page views older than 90 days. Both refuse to run without it. At
# least 16 characters.
# CRON_SECRET=""

# Log every SQL statement Prisma runs, at debug level, without bound
# parameters. Off by default: it buries every other line under a wall of
# SELECTs.
# PRISMA_LOG_QUERIES="true"

# Connection pool for each API, agent or script process (packages/db). The
# defaults suit one long-running server: 10 connections, idle ones closed after
# 30 seconds, and a 10-second wait for a free connection. On serverless, where
# every instance has its own pool, lower the maximum so instances times this
# number stays under the database's connection limit.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX="10"
# DATABASE_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS="30000"
# DATABASE_POOL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"


# ── Anonymous usage telemetry ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This repo is MIT and cloned rather than installed, so there is no download
# count and no runtime signal. Once a day the API sends one event of counts —
# how many contacts exist in bands rather than exactly, which of the agent's
# tools were called, how the evidence ledger behaved, which optional keys are
# set as booleans — tied to a random UUID generated by the first migration.
#
# It is server-side only. There is no posthog-js anywhere, no autocapture and
# no session replay, because autocapture on a CRM would lift contact names and
# deal amounts out of your database. No name, address, company, subject line,
# amount, prompt or key is ever sent, and neither is your IP: every event
# carries $ip: null with geoip off.
#
# docs/telemetry.md names every event and every property that is sent, and
# lists what never is.

# Turn it off. "1" is the whole interface, and DO_NOT_TRACK=1 does the same.
# When either is set no client is created, so nothing can be sent later.
#
# There is no variable for where it goes: the project key and host are written
# into packages/telemetry, because a write-only key is not a secret and a
# variable would only suggest it were one. Change them there if you want the
# numbers in your own project.
# CRM_TELEMETRY_DISABLED="1"
